1
00:00:12,050 --> 00:00:38,050
Prior to starting work in a BSC, schedule uninterrupted work time if possible and collect all of the laboratory materials that will be needed such as absorbent pads, a biohazard waste container, pipettes, pipette tips, microfuge tubes, a vortex, a small sharps container, paper towels, and whatever else you may need.

2
00:00:38,050 --> 00:00:42,050
Place these materials on a cart next to the BSC.

3
00:00:42,050 --> 00:00:47,050
A plastic squirt bottle with disinfectant will also be required.

4
00:00:47,050 --> 00:00:53,050
For the purposes of this training, we are using a generically labeled disinfectant bottle.

5
00:00:53,050 --> 00:00:58,050
Use and label disinfectants based on OSHA requirements.

6
00:00:58,050 --> 00:01:05,050
For information on labeling and best practices, consult your supervisor or safety office.

7
00:01:06,050 --> 00:01:10,050
First, confirm that the UV light is turned off.

8
00:01:10,050 --> 00:01:14,050
Then, check the expiration date on the BSC certification.

9
00:01:14,050 --> 00:01:21,050
BSCs must be certified when installed, whenever moved, and at least once annually.

10
00:01:21,050 --> 00:01:27,050
Next, turn on both the fluorescent light and the blower motor.

11
00:01:27,050 --> 00:01:37,050
Confirm that the alarm button is on, and open the sash and set it to the proper height, as noted on the outside wall of the BSC.

12
00:01:37,050 --> 00:01:43,050
The BSC sash alarm will sound if the sash is set too high.

13
00:01:43,050 --> 00:01:52,050
Next, make sure the drain valve handle is perpendicular to the valve body, indicating that it is closed.

14
00:01:53,050 --> 00:02:01,050
Allow the cabinet to run for at least four minutes in order to purge the air of particulates inside the BSC.

15
00:02:01,050 --> 00:02:10,050
If the BSC has a pressure differential gauge, also called a magnahelic gauge, this needs to be checked and recorded.

16
00:02:10,050 --> 00:02:15,050
Compare the reading to what was recorded previously.

17
00:02:15,050 --> 00:02:27,050
Gradual changes will generally occur over time due to filter loading, but a sudden change in pressure reading indicates that there is a block or breach of the filter system.

18
00:02:27,050 --> 00:02:33,050
Perhaps most important, this reading should never be zero.

19
00:02:33,050 --> 00:02:40,050
If this happens, the BSC should not be used, and you should contact your safety office.

20
00:02:40,050 --> 00:02:47,050
Some labs keep a pressure gauge log taped on the BSC and record the daily readings.

21
00:02:47,050 --> 00:02:53,050
That way, you can see quickly if there has been a significant change from the previous day.

22
00:02:53,050 --> 00:02:58,050
Inward airflow should be verified prior to use.

23
00:02:58,050 --> 00:03:04,050
You can use a smoke generator, or you can tape a tissue to the bottom of the sash.

24
00:03:04,050 --> 00:03:09,050
The tissue should be drawn into the cabinet to show inward airflow.

25
00:03:09,050 --> 00:03:19,050
Once the cabinet has been running for at least four minutes, gather the necessary supplies for decontamination and place them inside the BSC.

26
00:03:19,050 --> 00:03:33,050
Decontaminate the inside using an appropriate liquid disinfectant that has been approved as effective against the infectious agents you are working with and has been designated in your laboratory SOP.

27
00:03:33,050 --> 00:03:38,050
Clean all of the surfaces with the liquid disinfectant and disposable towels.

28
00:03:38,050 --> 00:03:48,050
This includes the sidewalls, the back wall, the inside of the glass on the sash, and the work surface.

29
00:03:48,050 --> 00:03:57,050
It is not recommended to spray disinfectant inside the BSC because the mist can be drawn internally into the grills or HEPA filter,

30
00:03:57,050 --> 00:04:05,050
and this can cause pitting of the stainless steel parts inside the BSC, or it can cause damage to the HEPA filter.

31
00:04:06,050 --> 00:04:15,050
It is better to generously moisten the towel and wipe the surfaces, allowing enough contact time for the disinfectant to do its job.

32
00:04:15,050 --> 00:04:19,050
Contact time will vary depending on what is being used.

33
00:04:19,050 --> 00:04:24,050
Discard the used towels into the biohazard waste bag.

34
00:04:24,050 --> 00:04:28,050
Don't ever put your head inside a BSC.

35
00:04:28,050 --> 00:04:38,050
If you can't reach the back wall, use an extendable mop and then discard the used disposable mop cloth into the biohazardous waste container.

36
00:04:38,050 --> 00:04:49,050
If bleach has been used as a disinfectant, wipe the BSC surfaces again with water or 70% ethanol to remove any bleach residue.

37
00:04:49,050 --> 00:05:00,050
This is because bleach is corrosive and can pit the stainless steel, which can provide an ecological niche or environment for microorganisms to evade cleaning and disinfection.

38
00:05:00,050 --> 00:05:07,050
Once you have completed disinfection of the BSC, dispose of your gloves in the biohazard waste container.

39
00:05:07,050 --> 00:05:30,230
The stool or bench should be adjusted to ensure that your face is above the sash opening and that your armpits are level with the bottom of the sash.

40
00:05:30,230 --> 00:05:38,230
As a reminder to perform these tasks, a BSC checklist may be used according to your lab's protocol.
